I’ll inspect the diffraction data, metadata, and available crystallography tooling first, then identify candidate phases, build/refine the model in GSAS-II, and verify that all exported files are internally consistent. I’ll treat phase count and composition as open questions and report any ambiguity rather than forcing a phase assignment.

The chemistry points to the Mg–W–O system after a 700 °C reaction, but I’m not assuming complete formation of MgWO₄: unreacted WO₃/MgO or another magnesium tungstate must be tested against unmatched peaks. The scan is high-density Cu Kα data with both Kα components reported, so I’ll preserve the doublet in the instrument model rather than preprocessing it away.

Peak-by-peak matching now supports three crystalline phases: wolframite-type MgWO₄ is dominant; the resolved 23.12/23.57/24.38° triplet and its higher-angle companions identify monoclinic WO₃; peaks near 42.94, 62.26, and 78.88° require residual cubic MgO. I’ll now quantify these together and test whether any significant peaks remain unexplained.

The first quantitative trial confirms all three phases, but it also exposed two refinement pathologies I won’t carry into the deliverable: GSAS-II’s default sample-scale flag made the overall scale mathematically redundant, and unconstrained phase-size terms ran to nonphysical limits. I’m fixing the scale gauge and comparing simpler, physically valid profile models before selecting the final fraction set.

The comparison is still running through several constrained profile variants. The key acceptance checks are not just a lower Rwp: I’m rejecting any model with negative Gaussian variance/Lorentzian width, frozen-at-bound parameters, singular covariance, or unstable phase fractions.

A clear, physically interpretable texture effect is present in MgWO₄: a March–Dollase correction along [010] lowers Rwp from 9.09% to 6.68% with no singularity and explains the otherwise severe overprediction of 010 relative to 100. This changes the dominant-phase fraction by only about one percentage point, but it materially improves the structural fit, so I’m retaining it and checking whether the minor phases need analogous corrections.

The WO₃ preferred-orientation correction is only ~4% from random and improves Rwp by just 0.07 percentage point, so it is not compelling. I’m now testing a single WO₃-specific width term and the unknown effective polarization; either will be kept only if it converges away from bounds and leaves the phase fractions stable.

A single WO₃ isotropic microstrain term is strongly supported: it reduces Rwp from 6.68% to 3.82%, converges cleanly, and captures the broad WO₃ peaks that the sharper MgWO₄/MgO profile cannot. Because this shifts the WO₃ fraction upward to about 42%, I’m now explicitly comparing all plausible local WO₃ polymorph models under the same broadening treatment before finalizing the quantitative result.

The polymorph comparison is computationally heavier because each candidate is being re-refined from raw data, including its cell, scale, broadening, and the shared instrument terms. It is still progressing normally; no user input is needed.

The final candidates are still refining. Once complete, I’ll select by peak-position/splitting agreement and fit stability—not merely the lowest residual—then rebuild one clean final project so the GPX, CIFs, fractions, and exported profile all come from exactly the same refinement state.

The polymorph test is decisive. The accurate neutron-derived monoclinic WO₃ model (P2₁/c, COD 2311041) gives Rwp = 2.47% and GOF = 1.62, versus 3.52% for the older rounded monoclinic coordinates and 2.65% for triclinic WO₃; the triclinic refinement also relaxes toward ~90° angles and does not converge fully. The selected three-phase fractions are currently about 51.2% MgWO₄, 41.4% WO₃, and 7.4% MgO.

The clean final refinement has been rebuilt from the raw pattern and converged without frozen parameters or covariance singularities. It reproduces all 8,301 points with Rp = 1.88%, Rwp = 2.47%, and GOF = 1.62; the remaining residuals are structured around strong overlapping peaks rather than unexplained peak positions. I’m now doing file-level consistency checks and documenting the practical uncertainty.

Completed the three-phase Rietveld analysis:

- MgWO₄, P2/c: 51.19 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃, P2₁/c: 41.36 wt%
- MgO, Fm-3m: 7.44 wt%

Final fit: Rp 1.883%, Rwp 2.470%, GOF 1.620. Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases; practical systematic uncertainty is approximately 1–2 wt% for the major phases.

